cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite the manuscript below."
title: >-
  The Client as Interface Specification: API Attack Surface Recovery
  and the Validity of Negative Results in Web Security Assessment
abstract: >-
  A web application's server can only be reached through its interface, and the
  client that calls that interface is delivered in full to every browser. The
  client is therefore a recoverable specification of the interface. This work
  argues that for the vulnerability classes the OWASP API Security Top 10 ranks
  highest, that specification is sufficient both to form a hypothesis and to
  confirm it, and identifies a consequence for measurement: the specification is
  what makes a request well-formed, and a malformed request is observationally
  indistinguishable from an enforced authorization boundary. Includes a full
  reproduction package measuring the extractor against published OpenAPI
  specifications on three applications, two of them held out.
